.TH CBMAP_CONSTRUCT_SCOPED 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cbmap_construct_scoped \- declare and create an ordered map that is destroyed on scope exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

cbmap_construct_scoped(hm_name, key_t, val_t);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares an ordered map handle named hm_name, together with the companion
type variables that carry key_t and val_t to every later type-inferred
macro, and then creates the map with the default allocator and the default
order for key_t. The macro also attaches the cleanup attribute, which
destroys the map on every exit from the enclosing scope, an early return
included.

The macro is cbmap_construct(3) plus that cleanup attribute; nothing else
differs. Use this macro when the whole useful life of the map is one block,
and use cbmap_construct(3) when your code returns the handle, stores it, or
destroys it in a place that the compiler cannot see.

cbmap_declare_scoped(3) followed by cbmap_init(3) is the two-step form. Use
it for a map whose configuration comes from inside the scope instead of
from the declaration.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name  Name for the map handle to declare
key_t    Key type. It tells every later macro how to read a key, and tells
         the container which order to give the tree
val_t    Value type. Only the type-inferred macros read it; the library
         never gives it to the container
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. This macro declares variables and does not expand to an expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro stops the program through ccol_fatal_err() if the creation fails;
use cbmap_declare_scoped(3) with cbmap_init(3) when the caller must handle a
failure instead
The macro needs GCC or Clang, because it uses the cleanup attribute
The map keeps the elements in key order, and the walk visits them in
ascending key order. cbmap_begin_iter(3) describes that contract, and
cbmap_create_full(3) describes the order that each key type gets
For a struct key_t, the default order is a raw comparison of the
representation of the key, so use cbmap_construct_cc_scoped(3) for such a key
The exit of the scope destroys the map and clears the handle, so an
explicit cbmap_destroy(3) earlier in the scope stays correct
The companion type variables follow the scope of the handle, so a function
that receives the handle needs cbmap_redeclare(3) there
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
The handle must not outlive its scope. Do not return it, and do not store
it in a place that outlives the scope: either action gives out a handle
whose map the cleanup attribute frees at the exit of the scope.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
void report(int *ids, size_t n) {
    cbmap_construct_scoped(seen, int, int);
    int one = 1;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        cbmap_insert(seen, ids[i], one);
    }
    /* The walk visits the smallest id first */
    ccol_iter_declare(seen, it);
    for (it = ccol_begin(seen); it; it = ccol_iter_next(it)) {
        print_id(*ccol_iter_key_ptr(it));
    }
}                                 /* The macro destroys seen here */
.fi
